1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Composite indexes: column order matters. INDEX ON tickets (status, opened_at DESC)

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,600
tickets: stored in the order they arrived. Statuses are mixed together.

3
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:17,600
No index: scan all 12, keep the 5 open ones, SORT them, then keep 3.

4
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:28,100
12 rows read and a sort, to return 3 rows.

5
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,100
Sorted by status first. Inside each status, newest first.

6
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:35,100
Like a phone book: by last name, then first name within each last name.

7
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,000
With the index: jump straight to the first 'open' entry...

8
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:42,000
...read 3 entries already in date order, and STOP. No scan. No sort.

9
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,500
In the lab it's LIMIT 20 on 100,000 rows: the same idea, much bigger savings.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:47,800
Now filter only on the SECOND column...

11
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:51,300
The matches are scattered across every status group: no single stretch of the index to read.

12
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,800
Phone book again: easy to find "Smith, John". Hard to find every "John".

13
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:57,800
For this query, an index that starts with opened_at would fit better.

14
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:08,200
Remember: A composite index sorts by the 1st column, then the 2nd within it. WHERE on the 1st column + ORDER BY the 2nd: jump in, read in order, stop. Filter only on the 2nd column: matches are scattered. Choose column order from the real query, not a rule of thumb.

